  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 401 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWidespread cloud cover remains in place across much of the Northern \nPlains and Upper Midwest. Temperatures this afternoon are in the 60s \nwhere broken cloud cover is observed, with temps otherwise in the \n50s. Ridging continues to exit the area this evening with satellite \nimagery showing the development of a deep H5 trough moving into the \nsouthwestern CONUS. This trough will continue to the northeast, \neventually combining with an upper low moving southward out of \nCanada. This will be the source of widespread rain Monday through \nWednesday across a large part of the area. Cooler temps and breezy \nwinds are expected Tuesday into Wednesday, with another surge of \ncooler temps also late Wednesday into Thursday.\n\n...Heavy Rain Monday through Wednesday...\n\nWarm air and moisture advection ahead our next system support the \ncontinued mention of rainfall totals exceeding 1 inch across a large \nportion of the area. Best chances of seeing over 1 inch remain \nconsistently in southeast North Dakota and west central Minnesota; \nhowever, recent RRFS and ensemble analysis are hinting at a slight \nshift to the north. In either case, rainfall totals of 2-3 inches \nare possible where deformation bands form as the low inflects and \nmoves northeastward Monday through Wednesday. \n\n...Breezy North Winds Tuesday...\n\nMuch cooler advects into eastern North Dakota and northwest \nMinnesota late Monday into Tuesday as winds shift to the north on \nthe western side of the upper low. At this time, wind gusts look to \nremain just on the lower end of advisory level, ranging from 35-40 \nmph on Tuesday.",
